on" "q-51148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/state.json" "file_sha256" "8258eb37dbbd7a8d4213ea9c431e299ce27b8eba96c31bf2a029f955a927c8db" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-51148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/state.json" "file_sha256" "8258eb37dbbd7a8d4213ea9c431e299ce27b8eba96c31bf2a029f955a927c8db" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/state.json" "file_sha256" "8258eb37dbbd7a8d4213ea9c431e299ce27b8eba96c31bf2a029f955a927c8db" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-3x+1=(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{2}-3(2)+1 & =(A(2)+B)(2-2)+C({2}^{2}+3) \\\\ 12-6+1 & =7C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}-3(0)+1 & =(A(0)+B)(0-2)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 1 & =-2B+3C \\\\ 1 & =-2B+3(1) \\\\ 2B & =2 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(1)+1 & =(A(1)+B)(1-2)+C({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 1 & =-A-B+4C \\\\ 1 & =-A-1+4(1) \\\\ A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=2,$ $B=1$ and $C=1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}=\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/state.json" "file_sha256" "8258eb37dbbd7a8d4213ea9c431e299ce27b8eba96c31bf2a029f955a927c8db" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "18e02840c5d40a7706b746c87225c318d9d85bb25aa3cd2d829ce1113630a3da" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954117/state.json" "file_sha256" "8258eb37dbbd7a8d4213ea9c431e299ce27b8eba96c31bf2a029f955a927c8db" "basis" 616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap